WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:12.030
May 16 marks the centenary of the ratification
of the Sykes-Picot Agreement.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.230
The Sykes-Picot Agreement was the origin
of the modern Middle East.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.770
It was a secret agreement negotiated by Georges
François Picot and Mark Sykes, respectively

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:23.700
a French and a British diplomat

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:26.620
in order to divide up the Ottoman Empire in the Middle

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:29.410
East after World War I ended.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.800
The British were going to take what is now
Israel, the Palestinian territories, and Jordan,

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.370
southern Iraq as well.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.710
The French were going to take an area along
the Mediterranean, as well as much of Syria.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.159
And the Russians—who knew about this—were
going to take Istanbul and the Bosphorus.

00:00:45.159 --> 00:00:50.059
Now, the exposure of the secret agreement
was deeply, deeply embarrassing to the French

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:50.510
and the British.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.909
It was exposed after the Bolshevik Revolution
by the Russians, and it was almost like the

00:00:54.909 --> 00:00:57.379
Wikileaks scandal of the last century

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:02.079
because you had all the secret maneuverings
behind the scenes exposed, and the Allied

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:03.600
Powers had egg on their face.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.100
They promised the Arabs one thing and were
doing quite another.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.730
A hundred years after Sykes-Picot, should
the borders be redrawn?

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:16.660
Or are we stuck with the legacy of diplomatic
agreements a hundred years ago?

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:20.550
Let us know in your comments, and let us know
any other questions you'd like us to cover

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.530
in 60 seconds.

